; rfc-9964 - the AKP thumbprint inputs, COSE and JOSE
;
; DERIVED - not verbatim. RFC 9964 states these two parameter sets as bullet
; lists in prose (section 6) and ships no grammar of any kind. The bullet lists
; themselves are reproduced byte-for-byte in akp-source-renderings.txt. Do not
; cite this file as RFC 9964's grammar.
;
; Built from: section 6 (lines 240-279), section 3 (103-121). The structures it
; describes are those RFC 9679 section 3 hashes and RFC 7638 section 3 hashes.
;
; THIS IS THE FILE THE RECORD EXISTS FOR. RFC 9679 section 4.6 delegates the
; required-parameter set to each key type's defining specification; RFC 9964
; section 6 is the one specification that takes the delegation up, and it is the
; only defined COSE Key Thumbprint whose required set contains `alg`.
;
; Prelude names used (RFC 8610 Appendix D): int, bstr, tstr.

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; 1. The COSE Key Thumbprint input
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; Section 6, verbatim bullets:
;
;   *  "kty" (label: 1, data type: int, value: 7)
;   *  "alg" (label: 3, data type: int, value: int)
;   *  "pub" (label: -1, value: bstr)
;
; The map is CLOSED. That is the whole content of "the required parameters for
; AKPs are" read against RFC 9679 section 3 step 1, which builds a COSE_Key
; containing only the required parameters: a fourth entry is not a thumbprint
; input, it is a different document's digest.
;
; `pub`'s data type is the one bullet section 6 states without a "data type:"
; label - it gives `value: bstr` where the other two give `data type:`. Read as
; bstr; the IANA row (section 8.1.3.1, "CBOR Type: bstr") agrees.

AKP-COSE-Key-Thumbprint-Input = {
  1 => 7,                         ; kty, int, fixed at 7
  3 => int,                       ; alg, int - THE ENTRY NO OTHER KEY TYPE HAS
  -1 => bstr,                     ; pub
}

; The three sets this document registers code points for (section 8.1.1).
ML-DSA-44-Thumbprint-Input = { 1 => 7, 3 => -48, -1 => bstr .size 1312 }
ML-DSA-65-Thumbprint-Input = { 1 => 7, 3 => -49, -1 => bstr .size 1952 }
ML-DSA-87-Thumbprint-Input = { 1 => 7, 3 => -50, -1 => bstr .size 2592 }

ML-DSA-Thumbprint-Input =
    ML-DSA-44-Thumbprint-Input
  / ML-DSA-65-Thumbprint-Input
  / ML-DSA-87-Thumbprint-Input

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; 2. The JWK Thumbprint input
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; Section 6 gives a second set, for RFC 7638, as member NAMES rather than
; labels, plus an explicit ordering:
;
;   *  "kty"   *  "alg"   *  "pub"
;   Their lexicographic order, per Section 3.3 of [RFC7638], is:
;   *  "alg"   *  "kty"   *  "pub"
;
; CDDL over a JSON object is a rendering convenience, not the hashed artifact:
; RFC 7638 hashes a UTF-8 JSON string with no whitespace and members in
; lexicographic order. That order is NOT expressible in CDDL (see section 4).
; The exact byte sequence is in akp-parameters.derived.json and the three
; worked instances are in ../examples/vectors.yaml.

AKP-JWK-Thumbprint-Input = {
  "alg" => tstr,                  ; first in lexicographic order
  "kty" => "AKP",
  "pub" => tstr,                  ; base64url, no padding (section 3)
}

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; 3. The consequence, stated so it cannot be read past
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; `alg` is inside AKP-COSE-Key-Thumbprint-Input. For every key type RFC 9679
; section 4.1-4.5 defines it is outside, because section 5.1 excludes optional
; parameters and `alg` is optional for those types. RFC 9964 section 3 makes it
; REQUIRED here - "The alg JSON Web Key (JWK) parameter or COSE Key Common
; parameter is REQUIRED for all AKP keys" - so section 5.1 cannot drop it, and
; section 6 states the set outright so no derivation is needed.
;
; [analysis] Therefore one byte-identical `pub` under two of the three
; registered algorithms yields two unrelated 32-octet digests. The library has
; measured this on the document's own 1312-byte ML-DSA-44 key:
;
;   {1:7, 3:-48, -1:<that pub>} -> b8969ab4...d3a8059a  (printed by RFC 9964
;                                                        as the kid of Figure 6)
;   {1:7, 3:-49, -1:<that pub>} -> f507c417...f187315d  (computed here)
;   {1:7, 3:-50, -1:<that pub>} -> de68d5ec...e128f62b  (computed here)
;
; Full values, with the printed/computed distinction kept, in
; ../examples/vectors.yaml V-0007, V-0010 and V-0011.
;
; Why the document is not being gratuitous: AKP has no `crv`, and section 3 says
; "The parameters for public and private information classes contain byte
; strings in a format specified by the alg value." Without `alg` a `pub` has no
; defined interpretation, so there is no unambiguous COSE Key representation to
; digest. The requirement follows from the key type having no other
; discriminator.

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; 4. What this grammar deliberately does NOT constrain
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; (a) BYTE ORDER AND ENCODING DETERMINISM - the properties that actually define
;     a thumbprint input. RFC 9679 section 3 step 2 requires RFC 8949 section
;     4.2.1 deterministic encoding; CDDL describes maps as unordered sets of
;     entries and cannot express either rule. Validating a byte string against
;     AKP-COSE-Key-Thumbprint-Input therefore does NOT establish that it is a
;     well-formed thumbprint input. The rules live in
;     akp-parameters.derived.json.
;
;     For these three sets the order is 1, 3, -1 - encoded 0x01, 0x03, 0x20 -
;     which section 4.2.1's bytewise order and section 4.2.3's length-first
;     order agree on, every label being one octet. Measured: the whole input is
;     `a3 01 07 03 38 2f 20 59 05 20 <1312 bytes>` for ML-DSA-44 - map(3), 1,
;     7, 3, -48, -1, bstr(1312) - 1322 bytes in all.
;
; (b) THE HASH FUNCTION. Neither set names one. RFC 9679 section 3 step 3 takes
;     a hash function as a parameter; the one registered confirmation method
;     (RFC 9679 section 8) is SHA-256 and the Appendix A kid values reproduce
;     under SHA-256. RFC 9964 itself never writes "SHA-256".
;
; (c) WHETHER `pub` IS A VALID KEY. See akp-key.derived.cddl section 4(c). A
;     `.size`-conforming bstr of the right length is accepted here and may be
;     cryptographic nonsense; RFC 9679 section 7's uniqueness argument assumes
;     a valid key and section 7.3 of RFC 9964 requires the validation that
;     establishes it.
;
; (d) THE OUTPUT ENCODING. RFC 7638's thumbprint is base64url-encoded without
;     padding when written as a `kid`; the COSE thumbprint is a bare 32-octet
;     bstr. Neither is a structure, so neither has a rule here.
;
; (e) THAT THE TWO INPUTS AGREE. They do not, and cannot: one hashes JSON text
;     with base64url `pub`, the other hashes CBOR with a raw `pub`. The same key
;     has two different thumbprints, both printed by this document as `kid`.
;     Measured for all three parameter sets in ../examples/vectors.yaml V-0012.
